@@s039 Why it keeps the trees low
@@s040 Without precautions a tree can become a long chain, and <code>find</code> would cost <code>O(n)</code>. Two rules avoid it:
@@s041 
@@s042 <b>path compression</b>: during a <code>find</code>, each node met is linked directly to the root.
@@s043 
@@s044 
@@s045 
@@s046 
@@s047 
@@s048 
@@s049 
@@s050 
@@s051 
@@s052 
@@s053 The cost
@@s054 With <b>link by size</b> and <b>path compression</b> together, <code>m</code> operations cost <code>O(m · α(m, n))</code> in total, where <code>α</code> is the inverse of Ackermann: it grows so slowly that in practice it is a constant (≤ 4 for any real input). Without path compression but with link by size: <code>O(log n)</code> per operation.
